            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Campaigns and Strategies</strong><br>\nOne of Bernays's most famous campaigns was for the American Tobacco Company. He launched the \"Torches of Freedom\" campaign to encourage women to smoke cigarettes, which were then considered taboo for women. By linking smoking to women's freedom and suffrage, he changed public attitudes and opened a new market for his client.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Influence on Government and Corporates</strong><br>\nBernays also worked with the United States government. During the 1950s, he was involved in the government's efforts to promote anti-communist views. His work influenced not just the field of PR but also advertising, marketing, and political campaigning, shaping methods used to influence public opinion to this day.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Books and Writings</strong><br>\nBernays authored several books on PR, including \"Crystallizing Public Opinion\" and \"Propaganda.\" His writings provide insight into the techniques and philosophy of PR. He argued that the manipulation of public opinion was a necessary part of democracy, to manage and direct the desires of the masses.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
