          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Teaching and Legacy</strong><br>\nBernays taught courses at New York University, sharing his PR expertise with students. His legacy extends into modern PR practices, where his strategies are still studied and employed. He is credited with transforming PR from mere press agentry to a professional field requiring strategic planning and execution.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Criticism and Controversy</strong><br>\nDespite his success, Bernays's work has been criticized for manipulating public opinion and promoting corporate and political interests over the public good. His techniques have been seen as exploiting the vulnerable and contributing to the culture of consumerism.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life</strong><br>\nOutside of his career, Bernays was known for his extensive networking and socializing, which helped him build connections that were essential for his PR work. He was married to Doris E. Fleischman, who was a pioneer in her own right as a female PR practitioner.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
